   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the insurance law, in relation to certificates of insurance; and to amend chapter 552 of the laws of 2014 amending the insurance law relating to certificates of insurance, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S3681",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3681-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd Art 5 §§501 - 504, Ins L; amd §2, Chap 552 of 2014",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3681\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the insurance law, in relation to\ncertificates of insurance; and to amend chapter 552 of the laws of\n2014 amending the insurance law relating to certificates of insurance,\nin relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo make technical amendments to Chapter 552 of 2014.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Article 5 of the Insurance Law, enacted by Chapter\n552 of the laws of 2014. These changes would clarify that:\nprohibitions against falsely amending certificates shall be applicable\nto addenda to such certificates; personal liability insurance and\nproperty liability insurance certificate forms must be approved by the\nDepartment of Financial Services; and the penalty for violating the\narticle shall be $1,000 for a first offense and $2,000 for a\nsubsequent offense, following a notice and hearing.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides for a 180 day effective date, and\nprovides that Chapter 552 of 2014 shall become effective on the same\ndate.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, insurance producers are sometimes asked to alter insurance\ncertificates to show coverage which does not exist in the referenced\npolicy. This places insurance producers in an untenable position.\nWhile the Department of Financial Services has instructed producers\nnot to amend certificates to reflect coverages which are not included\nin the insurance policy, the law failed to provide a statutory\nreference which a producer could cite when refusing to do so at a\npolicyholder's request.\n\nChapter 552 of 2014 prohibited any person to request the issuance of,\nor to produce, a certificate of insurance that contains any false or\nmisleading information. This bill would make technical changes to that\nchapter to clarify the kinds of insurance certificates which must be\napproved by the Department, and to specify the penalty for violating\nsuch law. Additionally, this bill would extend the effective date of\nChapter 552 of 2014 from 90 to 180 days from passage.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediate, provided that it shall take effect on the same date as\nChapter 552 of 2014.\n\n\n",
